 "action_summary": "The owner asserted that instant learning and instant learning in new environments are already solved perfectly, that therefore anything on a computer is solvable, and separately that the system is only a lookup system whose fix is model weights, conceptual understanding, transfer and reasoning. Seven claims were scored against recorded measurement. Instant learning is real: 22 of 22 same-bug repairs from a stored rule. Instant transfer is not: 1 of 40 on QuixBugs as taught and 3 of 40 with every generalisation on, and 18 of 40 fixes could not even be stored. The claim that weights are required is unsupported -- ReasoningBank is training-free and gains 8.3 points absolute. The claim that the system is a pure lookup is half wrong: every one of the 607 recovered changes is cross-file by construction. The one place the owner was right about the tree is naming: five organs already exist for exactly these ideas (rule_scope_learning_v1, settled_admission_v1, rule_expiry_v1, skill_recheck_v1, operator_generalization_v1). New measurement this round: alpha_safe_rewrite generalises a demonstrated operator change across alpha-renamed code with no oracle, and on the same 607 applicable changes it adds 0.0 -- because the organ refuses 0 of 330 askings on a corpus selected for sameness, so there is no refusal to rescue. The null is the result.",
